  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nBand of showers, with a few embedded TSRA, is advancing across\nnorthern MI at this hour. This is just ahead of a surface cold\nfront, which extends from central upper MI to central WI. This\nfront moves across northern MI today, exiting the far se late \nthis afternoon. \n\nLightning activity has decreased drastically since convection \nwas back in WI. Near-term guidance argues for this weakening to\ncontinue (and accelerate) early this morning, as the low-level\njet continues veering and become more parallel to the incoming\ncold front. Expect the convection band to break apart and \ndiminish in coverage/intensity over the next several hours. By\nthe time we reach sunrise/8am, western Chip/Mack Cos should be\ncomfortably behind what's left of our precip (and thus dry), but\nthe rest of the area will contend with a chance for showers.\nSignificant mid-level subsidence/drying expands across the rest\nof northern MI this morning/midday. All precip should exit by\n1-2 pm, with a partly to mostly sunny day after that. However,\nheading into tonight, colder air makes a stronger push. 850mb\ntemps drop several degrees below 0C on Superior tonight. It is\non the dry side still, but better moisture with a trailing\nshortwave is not that away over northern Ontario. Some lake\neffect rain showers will develop into the eastern UP tonight,\nespecially Chippewa Co.\n\nPretty quiet again to end the work week and thru the weekend.\nSurface high pressure moves from sw Canada, to and across the \nGreat Lakes. A 500mb ridge axis, starting out from the central\nplains to Canadian prairie, will gradually work eastward. It\nlooks to be overhead by Monday. Though low-level southerly\nreturn flow will be with us thru the weekend into early next\nweek, the building ridge aloft will make it difficult to get \nmore than just a stray weak shower. But as the upper ridge heads\neast, better rain chances return Monday night thru the rest of \nthe forecast. Both the northern and southern streams have \nsignificant energy working eastward. The degree of interaction \nbetween these is not yet known, but there is increasing \npotential for one or more wound-up systems in the central/ \neastern states mid-to-late next week.\n\nAfter another mild day today (not at warm as yesterday for most,\nexcept near Saginaw Bay), a noticeable but brief cooldown occurs\nFriday. We turn that right around to well above normal again \nbeginning Saturday, into at least early next week. Temp trends \nby late next week look considerably less certain. But this \nforecast is still reaching several days into Nov without needing\nso much as a mention of that evil 'S' word.",
